Here is the commit message:
fix(imports): fix a static import bug (#1060)

Here is the root cause:
The bug originates in `MinimalImportScanner.java`'s logic for determining whether a fully-qualified field/method reference must remain qualified (i.e., not be replaced by an import) due to naming conflicts with existing fields, methods, or local variables in the current scope. The original implementation collected all qualified-name segments (package names, class name, field/method name) into a `HashSet<String>`, which does not preserve insertion order, and then checked **every** token against the set of field/method/local-variable names in scope. This meant that if *any* segment of the qualified name — including unrelated package name segments — happened to coincidentally match an existing field, method, or local variable name, the scanner would incorrectly conclude there was a naming conflict, causing a static import to be generated (or withheld) incorrectly, even for private static fields that should simply remain accessible without any import in their own declaring class. Additionally, the package-traversal loop lacked a boundary check for the top-level (unnamed) package, causing it to keep climbing up and collecting extra spurious tokens beyond where it should stop, which further corrupted the token set used for the conflict check. The fix corrects this by using an ordered `LinkedList`, stopping package traversal at the top-level package, and checking only the last (actual identifier) token instead of all package-name tokens.

